-- What a language server may change. An edit a server asks for (a rename, a code action, `workspace/applyEdit`) goes through one page-side function (`state/system/machine/lsp/edits.ts`), which is two steps, `plan` (every check, and the read of what the edit needs) and `commit` (the edit the plan describes): a file open in any tab of the page is edited in that tab, unsaved and as one undo step, and every other file is written by the server operation `lsp-edit` of `POST /api/ops` in one request, behind the write chain, with a history copy of each file of at most 5 MB (`WRITE_MAX_BYTES` of `src/lib/data/file-ops.ts`, the limit of the copy kind `lsp-edit`, since the registry and feed files a rename of a hub key changes are over the 256 KB of a save's copy), an atomic write, a read back and one `files` record that History undoes as one step. The two steps are apart so that an edit can be shown before it is made: the rename of a hub key, and any rename the reader accepted with Cmd+Enter, is held as a Refactor Preview ("Hub keys as links") and is made, with the checked edits only, when the reader applies it; every other edit is made at once. The request names each file with the SHA-256 of the bytes the page read when it planned the edit and the server refuses the whole edit when a file's bytes are not those, a file lies outside the served roots, under `node_modules` or under a folder whose name starts with a dot, in the hub's `secrets` folder or in `~/.oculus`, is `local.yml`, or is over 5 MB; the page posts first and edits its tabs only after the post succeeded. The page refuses the whole edit, naming the file, when a file is open in a tab of another page (the relay tells it, from the open tabs every page publishes on the channel `lsp:workspace`), is a library file, holds a create, rename or delete of a file, names a version that is not the tab's, or belongs to a document that is gone. A library file's tab is read-only, so no edit reaches it. The interpreter pyright runs