+- The language servers. A Python, C, C++, TypeScript, JavaScript, Svelte, YAML, Markdown, LaTeX or BibTeX file in a source tab is served by pyright, clangd, the TypeScript server, the Svelte server, yaml-language-server, the Markdown server or texlab, which the oculus server starts as a child process (`server/machine/lsp/`); the page talks to it over its one socket on the channel `lsp:<language>`, through Monaco's own language client (`ui/controls/editor/kit/lsp/`) over a transport of oculus's own. A server is the processes rule's exception because it is oculus's own command with oculus's own arguments: an argument array and never a shell, `process.execPath` with the `pyright`, `typescript-language-server`, `svelte-language-server`, `yaml-language-server` or `vscode-markdown-languageserver` package under oculus's own `node_modules`, and `clangd` and `texlab` from oculus's PATH (`texlab` also from `latex.texlab_path`, started by its absolute path with a PATH of its own that holds links to `latex`, `kpsewhich` and `tectonic` and never to `latexmk`, so it reads no latexmkrc). texlab serves the `latex` and `bibtex` tabs and refuses `.rnw`, `.jnw` and `.pnw` files, whose code chunks it would read as LaTeX; it never builds, never runs ChkTeX and formats nothing, and the diagnostics it reads from a build log are dropped before the page sees them; the ChkTeX findings of a LaTeX tab come from the ChkTeX linter. The YAML server also runs under Node's permission model (`--permission --allow-fs-read=<oculus>/node_modules`), so a modeline or a `$schema:` key that names a file on disk is not loaded; its schemas are generated from the snapshot build (`src/lib/data/snapshot/schema/`) and handed to it from memory by the relay's answers to its `custom/schema/request` and `custom/schema/content`, and `config/local.yml`, a file under the hub's `secrets` folder and a file under `~/.oculus` (the folders `withheldFolders` of `library/rules.ts` names beside the engine's config folder) are sent to no YAML server. The Markdown server (`vscode-markdown-languageserver`, the server VS Code's Markdown support uses, with `markdown-it` for the parse) runs under the same permission model and reads no file itself: it asks oculus for the parse of a text, the bytes, kind or entries of a path, the Markdown files of the root and watchers on the paths its links lead to, and the entry answers those requests inside the served root alone (`server/machine/lsp/adapters/markdown/`), so `config/local.yml`, a file under the hub's `secrets` folder or `~/.oculus` whatever letter case names it, a path outside the root or through a link out of it, and a file over 2 MiB are never given to it. The relay pulls its diagnostics 500 ms after a document opens or changes and when the server asks for a refresh; a mark is worded with the path from the root and dropped when its path lies outside the root or in one of those folders. The entry counts a span between single backticks that names a file or folder of the hub as a path (`tools/cockpit/src/cockpit/schema.py:61`), marks one that is not there with the code `oculus.no-such-path`, and opens one on a press with Cmd at its `:<line>`; a press with Cmd on a link opens the file at the `#L<line>` of the link, or at line 1, and on a link to a file that is not there opens nothing. A value in a hub file that names a key of another file is a link: the YAML entry answers its definition, its references and its rename itself, from the reference index of the latest snapshot build, because yaml-language-server follows an anchor to its alias inside one document and nothing between files ("Hub keys as links"). The TypeScript server runs oculus's own `typescript` (`tsserver.path`), never a repository's, with automatic type acquisition and tsserver's log off, so no `npm` runs and nothing is written into a repository. One entry per server holds what is known before a message is sent (`src/lib/data/prose/lsp-servers.ts`: languages, label keys, semantic tokens, format on type, Organize Imports, choice kinds, approval) and one holds how it is started (`server/machine/lsp/servers.ts`); nothing else names a server or a language. One server runs per page, root and registry entry, shared by that page's tabs of the languages the entry serves, so one clangd serves the C and C++ tabs of a root and two tabs never start two. A page names a document `oculus-file:///<root key>/<path>`, the root key percent-encoded twice so a `/` in it stays one segment, and the relay resolves it by the file's real path against the served roots, refusing a part that is empty, `.`, `..` or holds a slash once decoded, an absolute path and a link out of the root; each address is turned into the server's `file://` form and back, so a server never sees a root key and the page never sees a path of the machine. A file a server names outside every served root reaches the page as a library address, `oculus-lib:///<token>/<file name>`, the file name percent-encoded twice as the root key is. Only an answer to a request of the page mints a token: the relay makes it for that page, the file's real path, the root key of the document the request was made for and the language of the channel, and the server that named the file, so one file named by two servers is two tokens and the same file named again by the same server is one. A notification or a request of a server passes a library file only when the page already holds its token and drops the location otherwise, and no field of any message, a command's `arguments` and the keys of a workspace edit's `changes` included, carries a `file://` path of the machine. No token is minted for a path with a segment that starts with a dot, for a path under a folder `closed_paths` names, or for a path under oculus's own `config/` folder, `~/.oculus` or the hub's `secrets` folder. The one exception to the dot rule is a file under the `sys.prefix` or `sys.base_prefix` that the pyright interpreter probe reports, minted for the pyright server alone, since a Python library lies under a version manager's or a virtual environment's dot folder; a prefix that is the home folder, the filesystem root or a folder that holds the home folder gives no exception. The page reads a library file only over the `lsp` channel with `read`, and the relay answers only for a token of that page: with the file's text, or with a code when the root is no longer served, the root or channel language is not the token's, the real path is no longer the stored regular file, the file is not text, or the file is over the limit of a source read. A token lives as long as the page's connection, and another page holds none of it.
